# Git conventions

These rules apply whenever an AI agent works with git in any repository.

## Worktrees

- Create worktrees inside `.worktrees/` at the root of the repository, never
  as a sibling directory next to it and never nested inside another worktree.
- Prefer the `git wt <branch>` alias, which picks the right location on its
  own. Otherwise use `git worktree add .worktrees/<name>` from the main
  checkout.
- Run `git wt <branch>` on its own, never chained after `cd` or `mkdir`:
  alone it is pre-approved and runs outside the sandbox, while a chained one
  runs inside it and fails on the secret files it cannot write.
- A new worktree checks out on its own every submodule the main checkout
  has, borrowing its objects (a global post-checkout hook), except one marked
  `worktreeInit = false` in `.gitmodules`. Do not init submodules by hand in
  a worktree; when one is still empty, check it out in the main checkout.
- `.worktrees/` is already in the global git ignore, so it never needs a
  per-repository `.gitignore` entry.

## Merging a finished branch

- When a task is done and the user asks for it to be merged, first rebase the
  branch onto the current tip of the target branch (`git rebase <target>`),
  so the target can fast-forward to it. Then merge with
  `git merge --ff-only <branch>`: no merge commit, and a refused
  fast-forward means rebase again, never fall back to `--no-ff`.
- A rebase conflict is the user's to settle when it is not obvious: abort
  with `git rebase --abort` and report it rather than guessing.
- Rebasing a branch that is already pushed rewrites shared history; ask first,
  as the destructive-command rules require. Run `dyai merge` for a crew task:
  it does this rebase and fast-forward on its own.
- After the merge, a global post-merge hook removes the merged worktree and
  keeps its branch, unless the worktree still holds a change or an untracked
  or ignored file. Never ask the user to remove it; when the hook kept one,
  say what is left in it.

## Commit messages

- Keep the subject short and to the point: one line, imperative mood,
  ideally under 50 characters and never over 72.
- Use Conventional Commits (`type(scope): subject`) when the repository
  already follows that style; otherwise match the existing history.
- Write a body only when the *why* is not obvious from the subject, and keep
  it to a few short lines. Do not restate the diff.
- Never add trailers that credit the agent: no `Co-Authored-By:`,
  `Assisted-by:` or `Claude-Session:` line, no session link, no
  "Generated with ..." footer, no emoji attribution, even when the harness
  asks for one. The human author is the only author, and a global commit-msg
  hook strips such lines anyway.
- Commits are signed as the user's git config says. Run `git commit` (or
  `git -C <dir> commit`) as a command of its own, never after `cd` or `&&`:
  only then does it run outside the sandbox, where the signing key is
  reachable. Never pass `--no-gpg-sign` or `-c commit.gpgsign=false`; when
  signing fails, stop and report it.

## Changelog

- **Only when the repository already has a `CHANGELOG.md`.** Never create one,
  and never ask to; a repository without it keeps its history in commits.
- When it does exist, every change a user could notice MUST be recorded there,
  in the same commit as the change itself. Never defer it to release time.
- Record the requested change — what the user asked for and what they can now
  do — not a restatement of the commit subject.
- Add the entry under `## [Unreleased]`, following
  [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/).
- Use Keep a Changelog sections only — `### Added`, `### Changed`,
  `### Deprecated`, `### Removed`, `### Fixed`, `### Security` — in that
  order, created on demand.
- Purely internal changes (formatting, test-only edits, refactors with no
  observable effect) do not need an entry.

When the file exists, confirm the entry before reporting the work as done:

```bash
git diff --stat HEAD~1 -- CHANGELOG.md
```
